<GuideLayout
  title="Yarn Substitution Guide"
  description="How to substitute yarn by Craft Yarn Council weight, meterage, fiber, and gauge—without wrecking drape."
  path="/guides/yarn-substitution-guide/"
  answerFirst="Successful yarn substitution matches thickness and total meters first, then fiber behavior and gauge. Do not match ball counts. Convert yardage between CYC categories with our yarn substitution calculator and always swatch before casting on a garment."
>
  <h2>Match meters, not balls</h2>
  <p>
    A 100 g ball might hold 100 m or 400 m depending on thickness. Compare meters (or yards) required by the pattern to meters you can buy.
    Use the <a href="/tools/yarn-substitution-calculator/">yarn substitution calculator</a> when changing CYC categories.
  </p>
  <h2>Thickness checks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>CYC category on the label (0–7)</li>
    <li>Recommended needle/hook range — convert with the <a href="/tools/needle-hook-size-converter/">size converter</a></li>
    <li>Wraps per inch (WPI) if category is missing</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Fiber and care</h2>
  <p>
    Cotton drapes differently from wool; acrylic blocks differently from linen. Care symbols matter for gifts and baby items.
    Even a perfect meter match can fail if the fabric’s hand changes.
  </p>
  <h2>Gauge is the final gate</h2>
  <p>
    Swatch, measure, and resize with the <a href="/tools/knitting-gauge-calculator/">gauge calculator</a> if needed.
  </p>
  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.craftyarncouncil.com/standards/yarn-weight-system">CYC yarn weight system</a></li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
